##DESCRIPTION
## Hooke's law; calculate force given displacement
##ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Mechanics)
## DBchapter(Oscillations)
## DBsection(Hooke's law)
## Date(26 December 2017)
## Institution(Brock University)
## Author(Santo D'Agostino)
## TitleText('')
## AuthorText('')
## EditionText('2018')
## Section('16.1')
## Problem('002')
## KEYWORDS(Hooke's law, mechanics, spring)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

$k  = random(200, 300);
$x0 = random(10,  20);
$x1 = $x0 + random(5, 9);
$x  = random(2, 5);
$F  = Compute("$k*$x/100");

BEGIN_PGML
a) A spring that has stiffness constant [`[$k]`] N/m rests at its equilibrium length of [`[$x0]`] cm. You gently pull on the end of the spring, stretching it to a length of [`[$x0+$x]`] cm, where you hold the spring at rest. Determine the magnitude of the force that you need to hold the spring at its new length.

    [_]{$F} N

b) A spring that has stiffness constant [`[$k]`] N/m rests at its equilibrium length of [`[$x1]`] cm. You gently pull on the end of the spring, stretching it to a length of [`[$x0+$x]`] cm, where you hold the spring at rest. Determine the magnitude of the force that you need to hold the spring at its new length.
  
    [_]{$F} N

c) Compare and contrast the results of Parts (a) and (b) and summarize your conclusions in a sentence or two.
END_PGML

ENDDOCUMENT();
